#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/id #uuid "7ad46566-b122-5de3-9b87-9beec9ac6761" :question/math-academy-id "e-21877" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.25\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 3\\sin(6t)\\,\\textrm{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nmy'' = -ky + F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.25y'' = - ky + 3\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 4ky = 12\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos \\omega t + B\\sin \\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega$ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4k\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=12\\sin(6t),$ which is $6,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2 = \\dfrac14\\cdot6^2 = 9 \\,\\textrm{kg/s}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "f41473ff-05a6-5f3e-ae5b-0584810aa0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21878" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/id #uuid "bb405211-5b18-5cc1-a264-bdf6e2fbcc60" :question/math-academy-id "e-6735" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5.$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$ Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e37a455-9027-5627-9c86-db06bc53bfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315813} {:answer/id #uuid "a9db326e-8624-5444-9c19-df9a1df02461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316075} {:answer/id #uuid "b876628a-2589-53f6-bc1f-24ea8ce077de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316163} {:answer/id #uuid "c9543bf6-5c41-5116-a38b-079703636f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316253} {:answer/id #uuid "e20b73e0-5256-567b-99f7-cd5a568f8b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9db326e-8624-5444-9c19-df9a1df02461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316075} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5bb2e0-324d-59c3-81bd-e27810cd574c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc1701ab-6384-5d15-af29-a24b10d78b11" :question/math-academy-id "q-343662" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de9bbff4-d11d-5ce4-abf2-01d269848931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9bbff4-d11d-5ce4-abf2-01d269848931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316352} :answer-field/id #uuid "d350858b-5387-5d1a-9e40-018edc34bd55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b200d20b-8850-5ea9-88a8-3f31357f34c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343667" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.6{y}^{″}=-ky-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{3}y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{3}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427253} :answer-field/id #uuid "69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" :question/math-academy-id "q-343685" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427246} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" :question/math-academy-id "q-343697" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b62b4-35bf-5f65-a234-731b87e6b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315226} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f7e9f-bc9a-5156-9e6c-eb2d65f5be02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316097} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cd1ed2-dbe0-5e10-b9da-7589b908f3f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b48322-3a21-5534-beec-3bbaaba87080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316332} {:answer/id #uuid "f02e5512-71d9-56b4-aef2-9cbbc519c597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f7e9f-bc9a-5156-9e6c-eb2d65f5be02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316097} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab88447e-79ce-5c0e-83ae-9cdc9dc98f0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3afec700-f2aa-5108-b688-bd4de9c948ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-343756" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20689423-7313-5d46-a6cf-e53b45d5b611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315300} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa03f4f-6c69-5a85-94cd-4de66372c7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315455} {:answer/id #uuid "97d16c26-30a9-55c4-9001-27d940379caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315964} {:answer/id #uuid "98f94d21-6aee-5c49-b84e-260bc8160b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315972} {:answer/id #uuid "ea08d6fe-ee76-5fc4-900c-60f312994799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97d16c26-30a9-55c4-9001-27d940379caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7ddf4a-aa47-5ee3-989e-8211dea84884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4e3c955-6b4a-5e2d-8d23-b51d3a994a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343763" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $5.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =5.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =5$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(10D-25Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-(10C+25Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)]+25t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 10D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-10C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6836f793-bf1a-5237-ba04-8ed876bf02a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315703} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7c86d8-3fdb-5039-9555-48fa4ef9e279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315723} {:answer/id #uuid "83f8e887-b53d-5a3e-a629-2ac440293b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315850} {:answer/id #uuid "8ded1f44-491f-5f68-af42-207c304eed5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315915} {:answer/id #uuid "fc04ea61-c647-5f58-acb4-5985afa33db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc04ea61-c647-5f58-acb4-5985afa33db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316529} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d35e01-0301-53e3-be05-f39d3204cffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7120fcf0-d3de-513a-93a7-21786701d7c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343764" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6120990c-dd87-590f-9590-a3cccfefa184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333872} {:answer/id #uuid "67cb7725-deba-57f1-9c4e-bb8958890bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333941} {:answer/id #uuid "7524c61f-03fa-542b-9792-537e1b0aff14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334047} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb304a4-9c70-5db5-baef-50819e9100dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334387} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b04ce6-0d22-5ddb-aaca-e9709a6604d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb304a4-9c70-5db5-baef-50819e9100dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334387} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fb1da30-fb1a-5be4-af3c-533bece1f592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6af51c6-8a3b-5e30-952e-168721f14820" :question/math-academy-id "q-64650" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4a1d9f-f5d4-5edb-b00d-96e2b58ea65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427251} {:answer/id #uuid "467b4288-3365-5b2b-bc4c-ec1682758746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427252} {:answer/id #uuid "70fbbf9f-3577-55db-8dae-d950e945e12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427256} {:answer/id #uuid "cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427262} {:answer/id #uuid "e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427262} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50" :question/math-academy-id "q-94234" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$"}]}
